// Append-only log of `/sign-in/email` outcomes (issue #112) — and the lockout
// counter at the same time. The streak of consecutive `failed` rows *is* the
// counter, so there is no second, mutable piece of state that could drift out
// of step with the log it is supposed to summarise. Like `UserActivity` and
// `AssetActivity`, a row is written and never amended, so the model carries no
// `updatedAt`; the absence is deliberate.
//
// `email` is a plain string with no relation to `User`, and that is the point
// rather than an oversight. The attempt most worth counting is one against an
// address that has no account: a `userId` foreign key could not store it at
// all, and a nullable one would make "never had an account" and "account since
// deleted" indistinguishable. Keying on the submitted address also means the
// lock state carries no information about whether an account exists, which is
// what keeps a locked address indistinguishable from a wrong password.
//
// The address is stored the way `src/lib/sign-in-lockout.ts` normalises it,
// which follows how Better Auth normalises it for lookup — `dist/api/routes/
// sign-in.mjs` calls `findUserByEmail(email.toLowerCase())` — so a mixed-case
// retry lands in the same bucket instead of starting a fresh counter.
//
// This is restricted data: a list of addresses somebody tried to sign in with.
// No public query reads this table, and nothing in the application selects it
// outside `src/lib/sign-in-attempts.ts`.
//
// One index serves both queries the table has: the newest few rows for one
// address, and the retention delete for one address.

model SignInAttempt {
  id        String               @id @default(uuid(7))
  email     String
  outcome   SignInAttemptOutcome
  createdAt DateTime             @default(now())

  @@index([email, createdAt])
  @@map("sign_in_attempt")
}
